  - m_Id: 1891455281
    m_Localized: The screen is moving toward a sharp real image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455282
    m_Localized: 'Red: parallel to the axis, then through the focus'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455283
    m_Localized: 'Green: through the optical center, direction unchanged'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455284
    m_Localized: 'Blue: through the focus, then parallel to the axis'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455285
    m_Localized: u > 2f, inverted diminished real image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455286
    m_Localized: u = 2f, inverted same-size real image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455287
    m_Localized: f < u < 2f, inverted magnified real image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455288
    m_Localized: u = f, refracted rays are parallel, no image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455289
    m_Localized: u < f, upright magnified virtual image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455290
    m_Localized: Focal length is decreasing, so the lens converges more
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455291
    m_Localized: Step 1 / 8    The optical bench
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455292
    m_Localized: Step 2 / 8    Three special rays
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455293
    m_Localized: Step 3 / 8    Beyond 2f
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455294
    m_Localized: Step 4 / 8    At 2f
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455295
    m_Localized: Step 5 / 8    Between f and 2f
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455296
    m_Localized: Step 6 / 8    At the focus
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455297
    m_Localized: Step 7 / 8    Inside the focus
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455298
    m_Localized: Step 8 / 8    Changing the focal length
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455299
    m_Localized: The bench holds a candle, a convex lens, and a screen. The candle
      is the object, the lens converges the light, and the screen catches the real
      image. Object distance u, image distance v, and focal length f satisfy 1/f
      = 1/u + 1/v. The real image is sharpest when the screen is at the image.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455300
    m_Localized: A convex lens converges light. A ray parallel to the axis passes
      through the focus after refraction. A ray through the optical center does not
      change direction. A ray through the focus leaves parallel to the axis. The
      image is where any two of these rays meet.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455301
    m_Localized: 'When the object is beyond twice the focal length (u > 2f), the
      image is real, inverted, and diminished. It falls on the other side of the
      lens, between f and 2f. A camera works this way: a distant scene becomes a
      smaller real image.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455302
    m_Localized: When the object is exactly at 2f, the image is real, inverted, and
      the same size. It also sits at 2f on the other side. Object and image distances
      are equal, and the magnification is 1.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455303
    m_Localized: When the object is between f and 2f, the image is real, inverted,
      and magnified. It forms beyond 2f. Projectors use this magnified real image.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455304
    m_Localized: When the object is at the focus (u = f), the refracted rays are
      parallel and never meet, so no image forms. No screen position gives a sharp
      image.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455305
    m_Localized: When the object is inside the focus (u < f), the refracted rays
      diverge. Extended backward, they meet on the object's side as an upright, magnified
      virtual image. A virtual image cannot be caught on a screen; you see it by
      looking through the lens. A magnifying glass works this way.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455306
    m_Localized: The smaller the focal length, the more strongly a convex lens converges
      light. With the object fixed, a shorter focal length pulls the image closer
      to the lens and changes its size. A longer focal length converges light more
      weakly.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455307
    m_Localized: Step 1 / 4    The optical bench
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455308
    m_Localized: Step 2 / 4    Three special rays
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455309
    m_Localized: Step 3 / 4    Object distance and the image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455310
    m_Localized: Step 4 / 4    Focal length
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455311
    m_Localized: Drag the candle and the screen. A convex lens converges light. u,
      v, and f are shown on the right.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455312
    m_Localized: Red runs parallel to the axis and then through the focus. Green
      goes through the center and does not bend. Blue goes through the focus and
      then runs parallel to the axis.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1891455313
    m_Localized: Drag the candle in from far away. The screen focuses itself. Watch
      the real image, the missing image, and the virtual image.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649280
    m_Localized: Drag the focal-length slider. A shorter focal length converges more
      strongly, and both the image distance and the image size change.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649281
    m_Localized: Watch how the three special rays meet
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649282
    m_Localized: Drag the candle in toward the lens
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649283
    m_Localized: Move the candle, focal length, and screen freely
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649284
    m_Localized: As the object distance falls, the image changes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649285
    m_Localized: 'u < f: an upright, magnified virtual image'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649286
    m_Localized: The screen is close to the sharp position
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649287
    m_Localized: A sharp inverted real image is on the screen
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649288
    m_Localized: The screen is in the wrong place, so the image is blurry
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649289
    m_Localized: Sharp position v = uf/(u-f), about {0}
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items:
      - rid: 1134318009864945691
  - m_Id: 1895649290
    m_Localized: 'Hint: {0}'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items:
      - rid: 1134318009864945691
  - m_Id: 1895649291
    m_Localized: "1/v = 1/f - 1/u, so v = uf/(u-f) \u2248 {0} cm"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items:
      - rid: 1134318009864945691
  - m_Id: 1895649292
    m_Localized: Enter the image distance in centimeters
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649293
    m_Localized: Correct, v = {0} cm
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items:
      - rid: 1134318009864945691
  - m_Id: 1895649294
    m_Localized: Try the calculation again. 1/f = 1/u + 1/v, so v = uf/(u-f)
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649295
    m_Localized: 'Correct: {0}'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items:
      - rid: 1134318009864945691
  - m_Id: 1895649296
    m_Localized: Not quite. {0}
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items:
      - rid: 1134318009864945691
  - m_Id: 1895649297
    m_Localized: Challenge 1 / 3    Find the sharp image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649298
    m_Localized: Drag the screen until the inverted real image is sharp.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649299
    m_Localized: Drag the screen until the image is sharp
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649300
    m_Localized: Challenge 2 / 3    Name the image
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1895649301
    m_Localized: Use the object distance and focal length to choose the kind of image.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
